{
  "id": 20,
  "name": "density_modulated_regret_v2",
  "intent": "Density-modulated regret extends the parent's progress-scaled regret by adding an inverse cluster-density bonus. When the candidate is in a sparse neighborhood (low cluster_density, common in uniform instances or late-tour isolation), regret weight gains up to 0.10, helping break ties when distances are nearly equal. In dense clusters, behavior matches the parent exactly, preserving grid/cluster strength. All coefficients remain bounded: return <= 0.45, regret <= 0.40. The hypothesis: sparse regions are precisely where regret is the clearest differentiator, so amplifying it there targets the parent's uniform-instance weakness without diluting its dense-region strengths.",
  "tags": [
    "regret",
    "cluster"
  ],
  "code": "def priority(f):\n    dist = f['distance']\n    ret = f['return_distance']\n    prog = f['progress']\n    regret = f['regret']\n    density = f['cluster_density']\n    return_coeff = 0.20 + 0.25 * prog\n    regret_coeff = 0.05 + 0.25 * prog + 0.10 * (1.0 - density)\n    return -dist + return_coeff * ret + regret_coeff * regret",
